# How hot are the world's hottest peppers?
subtitle: "Place four chillies on a ten-times-per-step heat scale, then learn why the burn is not real heat, and why birds do not feel it."

## How hot are the world's hottest peppers?
affirm: playful
text: {"font_size":34}
sources:
    - id: gwr
      title: Pepper X dethrones Carolina Reaper as world's hottest chilli pepper
      authors: Guinness World Records
      year: 2023
      url: https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/news/2023/10/pepper-x-dethrones-carolina-reaper-as-worlds-hottest-chilli-pepper-759706
      finding: Pepper X averages 2,693,000 Scoville heat units; the Carolina Reaper about 1.64 million
    - id: nobel
      title: The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2021
      authors: Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet
      year: 2021
      finding: David Julius used capsaicin to identify TRPV1, a nerve sensor that responds to heat
    - id: birds
      title: Molecular basis for species-specific sensitivity to 'hot' chili peppers
      authors: Jordt SE, Julius D
      year: 2002
      doi: 10.1016/S0092-8674(02)00652-9
      finding: bird TRPV1 is insensitive to capsaicin, so birds eat chillies and disperse their seeds

    icon chili size 120 x 890 y 600 icon_look picture of "hot pepper" hidden true
    icon bell size 120 x 110 y 580 icon_look picture of "bell pepper" hidden true
    text bell_l "bell pepper: 0" #2f6b8f x 300 y 580 font_size 38 hidden true
    scale shu cites ["gwr"] x 70 y 255 width 850 unit "Scoville units" min 1000 max 10000000 log true hidden true
    cards heat cites ["gwr"] along shu hidden true
        * "Jalapeño" icon.of "hot pepper" value 5000
        * "Habanero" icon.of fire icon.set twemoji value 200000
        * "Carolina Reaper" icon.of skull icon.set twemoji value 1640000
        * "Pepper X" icon.of collision icon.set twemoji value 2690000
    text trpv1 "capsaicin → TRPV1" #b5482e cites ["nobel"] x 300 y 590 font_size 42 hidden true
    icon brain size 120 set twemoji x 640 y 585 icon_look picture of brain hidden true
    arrow nerve #b5482e from.x 820 from.y 595 to.x 710 to.y 590 hidden true

    card title "How hot are the hottest peppers?"

Chilli heat is measured in Scoville units, and a sweet bell pepper scores zero. Each step on this line is ten times hotter than the last.
    draw shu chili bell bell_l parallel true

    draw heat

    ask "How hot is each pepper? Drag each card to its place on the Scoville scale." on heat store p tolerance 0.08 stage own
        right: All {p.total} close.
        wrong: {p.within} of {p.total} close.

A jalapeño is a gentle few thousand units.
    highlight heat_1

A habanero sits in the hundreds of thousands.
    highlight heat_2

The Carolina Reaper averages about one point six million: as hot as some police pepper spray.
    highlight heat_3

And since 2023 the record belongs to Pepper X, from the same grower, at about two point seven million.
    highlight heat_4

    erase bell bell_l

Yet none of it is real heat. The burn comes from capsaicin, which switches on a heat sensor in your nerves called TRPV1.
    draw trpv1

Your brain is told you are on fire. David Julius used chilli to find that sensor, and shared the Nobel Prize for it in 2021.
    draw nerve brain

    erase brain trpv1

Birds have a version of the sensor that capsaicin cannot switch on. So they eat chillies happily, and spread the seeds.
    icon bird cites ["birds"] size 130 set twemoji x 640 y 585 icon_look picture of bird
    text nofeel "feels nothing" #2f6b8f x 330 y 590 font_size 42

The burn may be the plant's way of choosing who gets to eat it.
    point at.ref chili gesture underline

